// Runtime/CheckedDivisionRuntime.maxon — the FALLIBLE DIVISION family (A1): the two callee names a
// possibly-zero `/` or `mod` desugars to, the error enum they throw, and the one ordinal they throw it as.
//
// ⭐ **WHY A "RUNTIME" FILE THAT INSTALLS NO RUNTIME FUNCTION.** Every other throwing builtin family here
// is a set of BUILT Std functions (`__arr_get`, `__mf_open_read`, `__md_next`, `__gt_process_run`), and its
// file holds the builders. This one holds none, because a checked divide is EXPANDED IN PLACE at the point
// of use (`LowerMaxonToStd.lowerCheckedDivision`) rather than called: the whole body is a compare, the
// `setcc` that materializes it, an `or`, and the divide the program asked for — so a `call`/`ret` around it
// would be a function whose prologue costs more than its work, the same argument `emitMonotonicMs` makes for
// inlining its divide instead of building a `__clock_now_ms`. The bootstrap reaches the identical conclusion
// (`MaxonToStandardConversion.ManagedMemory.cs:2336-2394` lowers `__checked_div` inline and deliberately
// calls no runtime helper).
//
// What the file DOES own is everything a family needs BESIDES a body — and every one of those is a fact
// some other file has to agree with, which is exactly why they live together in one:
//
//   • the two CALLEE NAMES, and the classifier that maps a name back to WHICH division it is;
//   • the THROWING predicate `isThrowingRuntimeCallee` composes (so `try` is accepted, and a bare divide
//     is refused with E3057);
//   • the `throws` CLAUSE `runtimeThrowsClause` answers with (so an `otherwise (e)` binding is typed
//     `__DivisionByZeroError` and `match e` dispatches on its cases rather than reporting a gap);
//   • the ORDINAL the lowering encodes, and the `BuiltinErrorOrdinalCheck` claim that holds it to the
//     declaration.
//
// ⭐ **THE PREDICATE AND THE LOWERING'S DISPATCH ARE ONE LIST, DERIVED, NOT TWO LISTS CHECKED.** The other
// four families need a bidirectional install-time guard (`ArrayRuntime.verifyThrowingAccessorsMatchPredicate`)
// because a name-listing predicate and a set of builders that stamp a `throws` clause are genuinely two
// copies of one fact, and only a check can hold them together. Here there is nothing to check: the two name
// CONSTANTS below are the only place either name is WRITTEN, `divisionKindOf` is the only classifier over
// them, `isThrowingDivisionRuntimeCallee` is DERIVED from its answer, and `lowerCheckedDivision` matches that
// same answer EXHAUSTIVELY — so a third kind cannot be named as throwing without the lowering refusing to
// compile, and cannot be expanded without being named. A guard over one list would be a check with nothing on
// the other side of it. (Ordinal drift is a different fact with a different pair of copies, and it DOES get
// its claim below.)
let CheckedDivideName = b"__checked_div"
let CheckedModuloName = b"__checked_mod"

// ⭐ **WHICH DIVISION A CHECKED-DIVISION CALLEE NAMES** — the one list, read three ways (the throwing
// predicate below, the E3057 message's wording, and the Std expansion's opcode choice). `notCheckedDivision`
// is the ordinary answer for every other callee in the program, so this is asked on the general call path
// and must not allocate: both arms return a payload-free variant.
export union CheckedDivisionKind
	// `a / b` — the QUOTIENT. Integer (`StdOp.div`) or float (`binOp(div)`), decided by the operand type at
	// lowering, which is why one name serves both: the desugar is about FALLIBILITY, not about width.
	quotient
	// `a mod b` — the REMAINDER. Integer only; float `mod` does not exist in the language.
	remainder
	notCheckedDivision
end 'CheckedDivisionKind'

export function divisionKindOf(callee ByteArray) returns CheckedDivisionKind
	if callee.equals(CheckedDivideName) 'divide'
		return CheckedDivisionKind.quotient
	end 'divide'

	if callee.equals(CheckedModuloName) 'modulo'
		return CheckedDivisionKind.remainder
	end 'modulo'

	return CheckedDivisionKind.notCheckedDivision
end 'divisionKindOf'

// The callee name a source-level `/` or `mod` desugars to — the operator → name direction, beside the
// name → kind direction above so the two constants are the ONE place either is written. Its gate is
// `TypeRules.divisionOperator`, and `Parser.emitDivOrMod` is its only caller: any other operator here is a
// routing bug in the expression climber, so it panics rather than defaulting to the quotient (which would
// silently turn `a + b` into a division).
export function checkedDivisionCalleeForOp(op MaxonBinOp) returns ByteArray
	if op == MaxonBinOp.div 'divide'
		return CheckedDivideName
	end 'divide'

	if op == MaxonBinOp.mod 'modulo'
		return CheckedModuloName
	end 'modulo'

	panic("checkedDivisionCalleeForOp: '{op.rawValue}' is not a division operator — `divisionOperator` is the gate and `emitDivOrMod` its only caller")
end 'checkedDivisionCalleeForOp'

// The kind → OPERATOR direction, which closes the cycle the two functions above open (operator → name →
// kind) and is what lets `lowerCheckedDivision` hand the divide back to `lowerBinOp` — the ONE place that
// decides what a `/` or a `mod` becomes — instead of spelling `StdOp.div` / `StdOp.mod` a second time.
//
// ⚠ It is an EXHAUSTIVE match, deliberately: a third member of `CheckedDivisionKind` stops this function
// compiling, which is the only thing that can stop a new checked division from being lowered as a quotient
// by omission. `notCheckedDivision` is not a division and has no operator, so it panics rather than
// defaulting to one — `lowerTryCall`'s own match is what keeps it unreachable.
export function divisionOperatorForKind(kind CheckedDivisionKind) returns MaxonBinOp
	match kind 'k'
		quotient then return MaxonBinOp.div
		remainder then return MaxonBinOp.mod
		notCheckedDivision then panic("divisionOperatorForKind: `notCheckedDivision` names no operator — `lowerTryCall` routes only the two division kinds into the expansion")
	end 'k'
end 'divisionOperatorForKind'

// The THROWING checked-division callees (A1) — the SIXTH composer behind `GtRuntime.isThrowingRuntimeCallee`,
// and the only one whose whole family throws by definition: a checked divide exists ONLY because its divisor
// could be zero. A divisor the compiler proves non-zero never reaches a call at all (`Parser.emitDivOrMod`
// leaves it a bare `binOp`), so there is no non-throwing member for this predicate to exclude.
export function isThrowingDivisionRuntimeCallee(callee ByteArray) returns bool
	return match divisionKindOf(callee) 'k'
		quotient to remainder gives true
		notCheckedDivision gives false
	end 'k'
end 'isThrowingDivisionRuntimeCallee'

// What a checked divide throws — this family's speller, beside the four in `ManagedFileRuntime`,
// `ManagedDirectoryRuntime` and `ArrayRuntime`, and read through the one `GtRuntime.runtimeThrowsClause`.
export function divisionByZeroThrows() returns ThrowsClause
	return ThrowsClause.throwing(String.from(DivisionByZeroErrorTypeName))
end 'divisionByZeroThrows'

// ⭐ **THE ONE CASE, AND THE FLAG DERIVED FROM IT.** `__DivisionByZeroError` has exactly one case, so its
// ordinal is 0 and its error flag is `0 + ErrorFlagOrdinalBias` — which is 1, which is exactly what an
// `x == 0` compare already produces. That coincidence is what makes the expansion branchless: the divisor
// test IS the error flag, with no `select` and no second constant. It is also FRAGILE in the one way a
// derived value can be, so `lowerCheckedDivision` asserts it rather than assuming it (see there).
export let DivisionByZeroOrdinal = 0 as ParsedInt
export let DivisionByZeroFlag = DivisionByZeroOrdinal + ErrorFlagOrdinalBias

// The `const` payload the divisor is tested against, in BOTH domains: the integer 0 and the IEEE-754 pattern
// for `+0.0` are the same 64 bits, so one constant serves the integer and the float arm of the expansion and
// neither has to spell a bare literal. (`-0.0` is a DIFFERENT pattern and is caught anyway — the float arm
// compares as f64, and IEEE-754 makes the two zeros equal.)
export let DivisorZeroPattern = 0 as ParsedInt

// ⭐ **THE ORDINAL DRIFT GUARD (7b), FOR THE FIFTH FAMILY.** The enum's case ORDER is this family's wire
// format and `DivisionByZeroOrdinal` above is a transcription of it, so the two are asserted to agree on
// every compile — exactly as the four `install*Runtime` guards do, and for the same reason: drift is
// otherwise SILENT, because every `otherwise (e)` arm would simply name the wrong case.
//
// ONE case is not an excuse to skip the claim; it is the cheapest possible claim, and a family that does not
// make one reintroduces precisely the drift 7b closed. It is a free function rather than the tail of an
// `install…` because this family installs nothing — the caller is `Compiler.maxon`, beside the four installs
// whose own claims run there transitively.
export function verifyDivisionByZeroOrdinals()
	let divisionErrors = BuiltinErrorOrdinalCheck.create(builtinDivisionByZeroErrorEnum(), installer: b"lowerCheckedDivision")
	divisionErrors.claim(DivisionByZeroCaseName, ordinal: DivisionByZeroOrdinal)
end 'verifyDivisionByZeroOrdinals'
